course: dictionary
lesson: 10972
title: Many meanings of war
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A war is a long fight between countries or large groups, using soldiers and weapons. «War means killing and destruction» tells you the word is about real harm, not a game. Ask yourself: which words in the sentence show what war is?
explain: Sometimes war is used in a different way. «A war on litter» means a big, long struggle against something bad. Here the fight is against rubbish, not people. «A price war» means shops keep cutting prices to beat each other, which is a long, fierce competition.
explain: Notice how we use the word. We say «a war» for one fight, and «war» on its own for the idea, as in «war and peace». We say «at war» or «go to war» when countries are fighting. Try swapping your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: A war is a long fight between countries or large groups, using soldiers and weapons.
fact: «War» can also mean a big struggle against something bad, like «a war on litter».
fact: Any long, fierce competition can be called a war, like «a price war».
fact: Useful phrases: «go to war», «at war», «after the war», «war and peace».
fact: Remembrance Day, Anzac Day and Veterans Day honour the people who fought in wars.
exercise: «War means killing and destruction, and the people who start or run wars often do not want to say that plainly.» What does war mean in this sentence? | A fun game played with friends | A quiet day at school | A new kind of sweet | *A long fight between countries, with soldiers and weapons
exercise: Two shops begin «a price war», cutting prices every day to beat each other. What does price war mean? | A quiet shopping trip | A fight between two countries | A day with no prices at all | *A long, fierce competition
exercise: Which phrase means that two countries are fighting each other? | *at war | a price war | war and peace | a war on litter
test: A school plants trees to fight pollution, and the head teacher calls it «a war on pollution». What does she mean? | A race to plant the most trees | A quiet walk in the park | *A big, long struggle against something bad | A fight with toy soldiers
test: Why do we hold Remembrance Day? | To buy new soldiers | *To honour the people who fought in wars | To celebrate the first day of school | To start a price war
exam: Which sentence uses war to mean a long, fierce competition? | *The two shops began a price war, cutting prices every day | Two countries went to war over their border | Everyone agrees that war is terrible | Soldiers fought in the old war for many years
